{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Refuted premise (as originally filed)\n\nFiled as: current `origin/main` fails the locked complexity gate because `close_ticket` in\n`transition_close.py` reports 19 against a recorded ceiling of 17, to be fixed by extracting\nalong a seam.\n\n**That is false, and the suggested remedy would have changed working code without unblocking\nanyone.** Verified:\n\n* main tip `07e76a1ce3`: ruff reports `close_ticket` at **17**, and\n  `.github/complexity-baseline.json` records **17**. `--check` on a clean checkout returns\n  `active=60 new=0 increased=0 stale=0`.\n* the sha in the report, `104dc4e10a`: code **19**, recorded ceiling **19**.\n\nNeither tree contains a 19-vs-17 mismatch. There is nothing in `close_ticket` to extract.\n\n## The real defect\n\nCI runs `--lock`, not `--check`. `lock_against_base`\n(`scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:455`) compares the CHANGE's baseline against **main\nTIP**: a REMOVED entry or a LOWERED ceiling is allowed; a RAISED ceiling or a NEW entry is a\nviolation needing an administrator.\n\nChange 1673 (merged `8f6b6c9356`) extracted the signing tail from `close_ticket`, taking it\n19 -> 17, and drained the stale baseline entry 19 -> 17. Lowering is allowed, so it passed.\nBut every OPEN change whose tree still carries the pre-1673 baseline now presents ceiling 19\nagainst main's 17 — which the lock reads as RAISING it.\n\n**Verbatim reproduction.** Feeding main-tip ceilings as `base` and `104dc4e10a`'s as `branch`\ninto `lock_against_base` returns exactly one violation:\n\n```\nceiling RAISED for 'src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py::close_ticket': 17 -> 19 —\nan administrator must override the gate to raise a ceiling\n```\n\nThat is the reported symptom, verbatim. The same call with a rebased branch returns ZERO.\n\nSo the gate cannot distinguish \"this branch RAISED a ceiling\" from \"main LOWERED it underneath\nan un-rebased branch\". It blames the branch for a change main made. This is not specific to\n1673: **every future ceiling reduction on main will break the fleet identically**, so the\nshrink-only ratchet currently punishes the shrinking it exists to reward. A change that never\ntouches the baseline is still hit, because it still carries the old FILE.\n\nImmediate operational unblock (no code, no admin): affected changes REBASE onto current main.\n\n## Direction\n\nCompare the branch against **`merge-base(main, HEAD)`** rather than main tip, so only entries\nthe BRANCH itself changed can violate.\n\n* `_fetch_base_ceilings_from_main` (`:494`) currently fetches `--depth=1`, which is too\n  shallow to compute a merge base. Fetch main with enough history, and if the local checkout\n  is shallow (`git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository`) deepen it before retrying.\n* Read the baseline at the merge-base commit and use it as `base`. `lock_against_base`'s rules\n  are unchanged — only the base it is given changes.\n* **Fallback direction matters.** If a merge base still cannot be computed, fall back to\n  today's main-tip comparison with an explicit note. That fallback is STRICTER, never looser:\n  it can only over-report, so an unavailable merge base can never let a genuine ceiling raise\n  through. Fail-closed posture is preserved.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An un-rebased branch carrying the pre-reduction baseline PASSES the lock after main\n      lowers a ceiling — verified by a test reproducing exactly that scenario\n- [ ] A branch that genuinely RAISES a ceiling relative to its merge base still FAILS —\n      verified by a test\n- [ ] A branch that adds a NEW entry still FAILS — verified by a test\n- [ ] A ceiling LOWERED or an entry REMOVED by the branch still passes — verified by a test\n- [ ] When no merge base can be computed the gate falls back to the main-tip comparison and\n      says so, never silently passing a genuine raise — verified by a test","title":"complexity lock compares against main tip instead of merge base, so ceiling reductions on main break every un-rebased branch"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786570671946756001,"uuid":"60b1ee82-b9a0-4feb-8ba6-883844b7381f"}